# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 has "all_contingent → each_sometime_not"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 have "each_sometime_not"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "everything is possible not to be" and "each thing that is possible not to be at some time is not" are false. Is S3, "But it is impossible for these always to exist, for that which is possible not to be at some time is not.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S8 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 have "necessary_exists → all_caused ∨ self_necessary"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 has "necessary_exists → all_caused ∨ ¬all_caused"; they differ when only "there exists something the existence of which is necessary" is true, which the second allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S8: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "there exists something the existence of which is necessary" is true and "every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another" and "there exists some being having of itself its own necessity, not receiving it from another" are false. Is S8, "But every necessary thing either has its necessity caused by another, or not.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 has "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "all_caused → infinite_regress"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 has "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "necessary_exists ∧ all_caused → infinite_regress"; they differ when only "everything is possible not to be" and "every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another" are true, which the second allows; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 has "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "all_caused → infinite_regress" and "necessary_exists ∧ ¬all_caused → self_necessary"
aquinas-third-way.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another, then there is a going on to infinity in necessary things which have their necessity caused by another"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 does not
aquinas-third-way.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another, then there is a going on to infinity in necessary things which have their necessity caused by another"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
